fact WorkItem(id: "WI-356", description: some(value: "Make the WI-343 provider-side requires check binding-precise and transitive (its two deferred open design points (a)/(c)). v0 check_provider_requires (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs) is base-level: it tests sort_provides(candidate, required_sort) on the spec BASE symbol only, so (1) BINDING-IMPRECISE — it ignores the bindings on the spec's requires R[sigma] clause; it does not substitute the provision's sigma and verify the carrier provides R at THOSE bindings, so a provider satisfying R at the WRONG bindings passes (false negative; the 'does not provide' diagnostic over-promises); and (2) NON-TRANSITIVE — it walks direct_requires_chain only, so Spec requires A and A requires B checks A but not B (a transitively-required gap slips through). DESIRED: substitute the provision's bindings into each requires R[sigma] and check that exact instance is provided, walking transitive requires. Likely route through the existing binding-aware/transitive resolve() SLD machinery (candidate_sub_goals_owned / collect_provides_candidates) instead of the ad-hoc candidate-set + sort_provides. TESTS: a provider satisfying a sub-spec at the wrong bindings must error; a transitive A-requires-B gap must error; existing wi343_provider_requires_test + stdlib stay green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-01T08:01:47Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-356", author: "user", content: "READY TO START (v0 check is on main; this WI replaces its base-level/direct-only satisfaction test with a binding-precise + transitive one).\n\nWHERE: check_provider_requires (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs:5162). The v0 inner loop (~5216-5232) is what to change: it walks direct_requires_chain(p.spec) and tests p.candidates.any(|c| sort_provides(c, required)). That is (1) BASE-LEVEL (sort_provides ignores the requires entry bindings sigma) and (2) the candidate-set is an over-approximation (carrier OR any binding value). It is also only as transitive as the resolver it delegates to (it does not, today).\n\nFIX (reuse existing machinery, do NOT re-implement): for each provision build its sigma = the spec type-param symbol -> the provision binding VALUE, read from the SortProvidesInfo spec view via unwrap_spec_view -> (spec_base, view_bindings); filter view_bindings to type params with is_type_param_binding(kb, k, spec_qn) (skips auto-bound op bindings). Then for each requires R entry of the spec from direct_requires_chain(kb, p.spec): skip EffectsRuntime (keep the existing skip); for each type-param binding (k,v) in the entry spec view, substitute the provision sigma into v with substitute_impl_params_alloc(kb, v, sigma) (exactly candidate_sub_goals_owned's inner loop at typing.rs:5112-5143); build SortGoal{spec_sort: R, bindings: substituted, carrier: None} and accept iff spec_resolves_at_bindings(kb, R, substituted) (typing.rs:9927) is true. spec_resolves_at_bindings does resolve(goal, empty_scope)==Resolved, which is binding-precise AND transitive (resolve_inner recurses through R's own requires). If not resolved -> emit LoadError::UnsatisfiedProviderRequires{carrier, spec, required} (unchanged).\n\nSo: the sigma replaces the Provision.candidates set; spec_resolves_at_bindings replaces sort_provides; transitivity is free via resolve_inner. KEY: sigma keys must be the spec param symbols as the requires entry references them (Ref/Ident/nullary-Fn), matching candidate_sub_goals_owned's impl_subst shape (substitute_impl_params_alloc matches by symbol). resolve takes &mut kb and allocates, so snapshot all (carrier, spec, sigma) BEFORE the resolve loop (v0 already snapshots provisions).\n\nREUSE: spec_resolves_at_bindings (9927), candidate_sub_goals_owned (5112, the substitution template), substitute_impl_params_alloc (5255), is_type_param_binding (5239), unwrap_spec_view, direct_requires_chain (8877), resolve (4532). Related binding-aware helpers: goal_from_requires_entry (3813), requires_entry_covers_goal.\n\nRISK: binding-precise is STRICTER than v0. A stdlib provision that passed base-level (some named sort provides R at SOME bindings) but lacks R at the SPECIFIC bindings would NEWLY error -> may surface a real stdlib gap to fix. Verify the real provisions still resolve: List provides PersistentCollection requires Iterable[C=List[T]] (List provides Iterable[C=List[T], Element=T] OK); geometry VectorSpace[Vec3,Float] requires Ring[F=Float] (Float provides Ring in the binding layer OK).\n\nTESTS (rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi343_provider_requires_test.rs; existing: provider_missing_required_subspec_errors, provider_with_required_subspec_loads, provider_of_requireless_spec_loads): ADD (1) a provider that satisfies the sub-spec at the WRONG bindings must error (e.g. Spec[V,F] requires Ring[F]; provide Spec[V=X,F=A] where A does not provide Ring but the carrier/another binding does -> v0 passes, binding-precise must error); (2) a transitive gap must error (Spec requires A; A requires B; carrier provides Spec+A but not B -> resolve recurses into A's requires B). Existing 3 + full stdlib stay green. VERIFY: scripts/test.sh -p anthill-core --test wi_tests -- wi343 wi325; then full workspace.\n\nON DELIVER: WI-356 unblocks WI-343 (depends_on WI-356, WI-357). WI-357 (element typing) is independent. Branch from current main (WI-343 v0 tip b823941). See memory project_wi343_provider_requires.", at: "2026-05-31T11:25:52Z")
